# Implicit Parameters { .heading-no-number }

> [!NOTE]
> Implicit parameters are an experimental feature in Koka. The design may change.
> Read the [Static Overloading](overloading.html) guide first — implicits build directly on that.

Implicit parameters let you mark a function parameter with `?` so the compiler can automatically
supply it from the surrounding scope using type-directed disambiguation. This reduces boilerplate
for common patterns like equality, comparison, and show — without requiring type classes or instances.

---

## The Problem: Explicit Threading

Suppose you want a generic list equality function. Without implicits, you must pass the element equality function explicitly at every call site:

```koka
fun int/eq( x : int, y : int ) : bool
  (x == y)

fun list-eq-explicit( xs : list<a>, ys : list<a>, eq : (a,a) -> bool ) : bool
  match xs
    Cons(x,xx) -> match ys
      Cons(y,yy) | eq(x,y) -> list-eq-explicit(xx, yy, eq)
      _           -> False
    Nil -> ys.is-nil

fun example()
  list-eq-explicit([1,2], [1,2], eq)  // Koka picks int/eq from context
```

This works, but becomes tedious when the equality function must be threaded through many layers.

---

## Implicit Parameters with `?`

Mark a parameter with `?` to make it _implicit_. The compiler fills it in automatically from
any in-scope function with a matching name and type:

```koka
fun list-eq( xs : list<a>, ys : list<a>, ?eq : (a,a) -> bool ) : bool
  match xs
    Cons(x,xx) -> match ys
      Cons(y,yy) | eq(x,y) -> list-eq(xx,yy)
      _           -> False
    Nil -> ys.is-nil

fun example2()
  list-eq( [1,2], [1,2] )   // ?eq resolved to int/eq automatically
```

In the recursive call `list-eq(xx,yy)`, the `?eq` parameter is resolved to the local `eq`
parameter itself — no manual threading needed.

---

## Propagating Implicits

When a function needs an implicit parameter it doesn't have locally, it can declare `?eq` to
let the compiler propagate it further:

```koka
fun list/eq( xs : list<a>, ys : list<a>, ?eq : (a,a) -> bool ) : bool
  match xs
    Cons(x,xx) -> match ys
      Cons(y,yy) | eq(x,y) -> eq(xx,yy)
      _           -> False
    Nil -> ys.is-nil

fun example4()
  eq([1,2], [1,2])   // resolves to list/eq (which then resolves ?eq to int/eq)
```

Implicit resolution is _recursive_: `list/eq` is resolved first, then its own `?eq` is resolved.

---

## Providing Implicits Explicitly

You can always pass an implicit parameter explicitly when needed:

```koka
fun example-implicits()
  // with named syntax:
  list-eq( [], [], ?eq = int/eq )

  // positionally:
  list-eq( [], [], int/eq )

  // with an expression:
  list-eq( [1,2], [1,2], !int/eq(_,_) )
```

This is useful when the implicit cannot be resolved automatically (e.g. for empty lists where the
element type is unknown).

---

## Multiple Implicits of the Same Name

When a function needs two implicit parameters that would both resolve to the same name (e.g. `eq`
for both element types in a tuple), further qualify the parameter names:

```koka
fun tuple/eq( (x1,y1) : (a,b), (x2,y2) : (a,b), ?a/eq : (a,a) -> bool, ?b/eq : (b,b) -> bool ) : bool
  eq(x1,x2) && eq(y1,y2)
```

Both `?a/eq` and `?b/eq` resolve using the unqualified name `eq`, but they are distinct parameters
with unique qualified names `?a/eq` and `?b/eq`.

---

## Conversions via Implicits

When resolving `?eq`, the compiler also considers functions that _produce_ an `eq` function given
further implicit parameters. This allows defining derived instances:

```koka
fun default/compare/eq( x : a, y : a, ?compare : (a,a) -> order )
  compare(x,y) == Eq

fun string/compare( x : string, y : string ) : order
  cmp(x,y)

fun example6()
  eq( ["hi"], ["there"] )
  // resolves: list/eq(_,_, ?eq = default/compare/eq(_,_, ?compare = string/compare))
```

The `default/` namespace is treated as lower-priority — if a direct `eq` exists, it wins
over a derived one via `default/compare/eq`.

---

## Implicits vs Type Classes

| | Type Classes (Haskell/Lean) | Implicit Parameters (Koka) |
|---|---|---|
| Resolution | Type-driven at compile time | Type-driven at compile time |
| Syntax | `deriving`, `instance` | `?param` prefix |
| Customization | Named instances, newtype wrapping | Pass explicitly at call site |
| Semantics | Can't run without types | Fully qualified names have untyped semantics |
| Complexity | Large design space | Minimal extension to existing overloading |

Koka's design goal is to be the _C of FP_ — powerful enough to express common patterns,
without the complexity of a full type class system.

